= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The defining <exponential function> formulas for the <hyperbolic functions> give
$$
\cosh^2x-\sinh^2x
=\frac{(e^x+e^{-x})^2-(e^x-e^{-x})^2}{4}
=\boxed{1}.
$$

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a <real number> $x$, the <hyperbolic functions> satisfy $\cosh x>0$ and $1-\tanh^2x=\operatorname{sech}^2x$. Hence $\cosh x=1/\sqrt{1-u^2}$, and multiplying it by $\tanh x=u$ gives
$$
\boxed{\sinh x=\frac{u}{\sqrt{1-u^2}},\qquad-1<u<1}.
$$
The sign of the numerator preserves the sign of $\sinh x$.

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Multiply numerator and denominator by the <complex conjugate> of the denominator:
$$
\frac{2-3i}{2+3i}=\frac{(2-3i)^2}{2^2+3^2}
=-\frac5{13}-\frac{12}{13}i.
$$
Thus the <real part> and <imaginary part> are $\boxed{\operatorname{Re}=-5/13,\qquad\operatorname{Im}=-12/13}$.

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

The <gradient> is $\nabla\phi=(2xy+z,x^2,x)$, with value $(3,1,1)$ at the point. Normalize the direction to a <unit vector>: $\hat{\mathbf s}=(2,0,1)/\sqrt5$. The <directional derivative> is therefore
$$
\boxed{D_{\hat{\mathbf s}}\phi=\nabla\phi\cdot\hat{\mathbf s}
=\frac{6+1}{\sqrt5}=\frac7{\sqrt5}}.
$$

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

The <characteristic polynomial> is $(1-\lambda)(4-\lambda)-4=\lambda(\lambda-5)$. Solving each <eigenvalue equation> gives the <eigenvalues> and <eigenvectors>
$$
\boxed{\lambda=0:\ \mathbf v=c(-2,1),\qquad
\lambda=5:\ \mathbf v=c(1,2),\qquad c\ne0}.
$$
For unit-length representatives divide each displayed direction by $\sqrt5$. The two eigendirections are <orthogonal>, consistently with the <matrix> being a <symmetric matrix>.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Put $h=x-\pi/4$. The <trigonometric identities> give $\cos x\sin x=\tfrac12\sin2x=\tfrac12\cos2h$. Expanding <cosine> at zero gives
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\frac12-\left(x-\frac\pi4\right)^2
+O\left((x-\pi/4)^4\right)}.
$$
The linear and cubic terms vanish, so these are the first two nonzero <Taylor series> terms.

