{parent=a}

For $r>0$, use the <gradient of a radial function> with $g(r)=1/r$:
$$
\boxed{\nabla\frac1r=-\frac{\mathbf r}{r^3}}.
$$
Equivalently each component is $-x_j/r^3$ by differentiating $r^2=x^2+y^2+z^2$. The origin is excluded from the <function>'s domain.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <product rule for divergence>, $\nabla\cdot\mathbf r=3$, and the result in (a) give, for $r>0$,
$$
\nabla\cdot\frac{\mathbf r}{r}
=\frac1r\nabla\cdot\mathbf r+\mathbf r\cdot\nabla\frac1r
=\frac3r-\frac{\mathbf r\cdot\mathbf r}{r^3}
=\boxed{\frac2r}.
$$
This is a pointwise identity away from the origin, where the radial <unit vector> is undefined.

