Using the <Einstein summation convention>, reorder the scalar components and sum over the two dummy indices separately:
$$
a_i b_k b_i a_k=(a_i b_i)(a_k b_k)
=\boxed{(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b)^2}.
$$
The repeated suffixes each represent the three-dimensional component sum.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Contract the <Kronecker deltas> in the first term to obtain $a_i b_i$. In the second term, $\delta_{ij}$ identifies the two $a$ components, while the two $b_k$ components already contract. Therefore
$$
\boxed{a_i b_k\delta_{km}\delta_{im}+a_i b_k a_j b_k\delta_{ij}
=\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b+(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf a)(\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf b)}.
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

In three dimensions each contracted delta is the <trace> of the identity, $\delta_{jj}=\delta_{kk}=3$. Thus the exponent is six and the base is $\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf a$. Hence
$$
\boxed{(a_i a_i)^{\delta_{jj}+\delta_{kk}}
=(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf a)^6=|\mathbf a|^{12}}.
$$

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

An <antisymmetric matrix> satisfies $A^T=-A$. Each diagonal entry is therefore its own negative, so both diagonal entries vanish. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{tr}A=0}.
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A real two-dimensional <antisymmetric matrix> has the form $A=\begin{pmatrix}0&s\\-s&0\end{pmatrix}$. Its <determinant> is $s^2=1/4$, and the specified positive upper-right entry selects $s=1/2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{A=\begin{pmatrix}0&1/2\\-1/2&0\end{pmatrix}}.
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Direct multiplication gives $A^2=-I/4$. Therefore $A(-4A)=I=(-4A)A$, yielding
$$
\boxed{A^2=\begin{pmatrix}-1/4&0\\0&-1/4\end{pmatrix},\qquad
A^{-1}=-4A=\begin{pmatrix}0&-2\\2&0\end{pmatrix}}.
$$
The nonzero <determinant> ensures that this two-sided inverse is unique as a <matrix inverse>.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Multiply $A^2=-I/4$ by $A$ once more:
$$
\boxed{A^3=-\frac14A=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1/8\\1/8&0\end{pmatrix}}.
$$
No additional <diagonalization> is needed.

= v
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Repeatedly using the quadratic identity gives
$$
\boxed{A^{2n}=\left(-\frac14\right)^nI,\qquad
A^{2n+1}=\left(-\frac14\right)^nA}.
$$
In entries these are $(-1)^n4^{-n}\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$ and $(-1)^n2^{-(2n+1)}\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix}$, respectively. This is the basic reduction for <functions of a two-dimensional antisymmetric matrix>.

= vi
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}

The even-power formula reduces every summand to a scalar multiple of $I$:
$$
\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{A^{4n}-2A^{2n}}n
=\left[\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{(1/16)^n}{n}
-2\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{(-1/4)^n}{n}\right]I.
$$
Both <series> have <absolute convergence>. Apply the <logarithm> expansion $\sum_{n\geq1}z^n/n=-\log(1-z)$ for $|z|<1$ to obtain
$$
\left[-\log\frac{15}{16}+2\log\frac54\right]I
=\boxed{\log\frac53\,I}.
$$
Thus the off-diagonal entries vanish and both diagonal entries have the required value.

