{parent=a}

Use <integration by substitution> with $x=2u$ and the Gaussian being an <even function>:
$$
\boxed{\int_0^\infty e^{-x^2/4}\,dx
=2\int_0^\infty e^{-u^2}\,du=\sqrt\pi}.
$$
The factor two from the differential exactly cancels the half-line factor in the given full-line <Gaussian integral>.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Retain the original PDF's full-line integration limits. For $n\geq2$, write $x^ne^{-x^2}\,dx=-x^{n-1}d(e^{-x^2})/2$. <Integration by parts> gives
$$
I_n=\left[-\frac12x^{n-1}e^{-x^2}\right]_{-\infty}^{\infty}
+\frac{n-1}{2}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}x^{n-2}e^{-x^2}\,dx.
$$
<Gaussian decay dominates every power>, so the endpoint term vanishes, so
$$
\boxed{I_n=\frac{n-1}{2}I_{n-2}}.
$$
This is the <full-line Gaussian moment recurrence>; its initial values differ from the half-line version.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The integrand for $I_1$ is an <odd function> with <absolute convergence>, so $I_1=0$. For $I_2$, the recurrence gives $I_2=I_0/2$, where the supplied <Gaussian integral> is $I_0=\sqrt\pi$. Hence
$$
\boxed{I_1=0,\qquad I_2=\frac{\sqrt\pi}{2}}.
$$
The zero first moment is specific to the symmetric full-line domain.

= c
