{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use <integration by substitution> with the reciprocal variable $t=1/x$, so $dx=-dt/t^2$ and $\log x=-\log t$. The endpoints $x=1,\infty$ become $t=1,0$. Thus
$$
\int_1^\infty x^{-3}\log x\,dx
=\int_1^0t\log t\,dt
=\boxed{-\int_0^1t\log t\,dt}.
$$
Renaming the dummy integration variable proves the identity without evaluating either side.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <integral> converges absolutely: near zero it behaves like $\log x$, and at infinity like $(\log x)/x^2$. Let its value be $J$. The <integration by substitution> $x=1/t$ gives
$$
J=\int_0^\infty\frac{-\log t}{1+t^2}\,dt=-J.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{J=0}.
$$
<Absolute convergence> makes this cancellation between the two ends legitimate.

= d
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <tangent half-angle substitution> $t=\tan(x/2)$ gives $\sin x=2t/(1+t^2)$ and $dx=2\,dt/(1+t^2)$. The endpoints become zero and one, so
$$
\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{dx}{2+\sin x}
=\int_0^1\frac{dt}{t^2+t+1}.
$$
Use <completing the square>, $t^2+t+1=(t+1/2)^2+3/4$, and integrate:
$$
\frac2{\sqrt3}\left[\arctan\frac{2t+1}{\sqrt3}\right]_0^1
=\frac2{\sqrt3}\left(\frac\pi3-\frac\pi6\right).
$$
Thus both integrals equal $\boxed{\pi/(3\sqrt3)}$.

