= Solution
{parent=a}

The <characteristic equation> has roots zero and three, giving $y_h=C_1+C_2e^{3x}$. Since the forcing is quadratic and the <homogeneous solution> contains a constant, use the <polynomial> trial $y_p=Ax^3+Bx^2+Cx$. Substitution gives
$$
y_p''-3y_p'=-9Ax^2+(6A-6B)x+2B-3C.
$$
Matching coefficients gives $A=-1/3$, $B=-1/3$, and $C=-8/9$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y=C_1+C_2e^{3x}-\frac{x^3}{3}-\frac{x^2}{3}-\frac{8x}{9}}.
$$

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The common <homogeneous solution> is $C_1\cos2x+C_2\sin2x$. For forcing $\sin x$, a trial $a\sin x$ gives $3a\sin x=\sin x$, so the first <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{y=C_1\cos2x+C_2\sin2x+\frac13\sin x}.
$$
For forcing $\sin2x$, the frequency produces <resonance in a differential equation>. A trial $ax\cos2x$ gives $y_p''+4y_p=-4a\sin2x$, so $a=-1/4$ and the second solution is
$$
\boxed{y=C_1\cos2x+C_2\sin2x-\frac x4\cos2x}.
$$
The <superposition principle> permits adding the two particular solutions. The sum-forcing equation therefore has
$$
\boxed{y=C_1\cos2x+C_2\sin2x+\frac13\sin x-\frac x4\cos2x}.
$$
Only one pair of homogeneous constants is needed, because the two original homogeneous families coincide.

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Differentiate $y'=-y+3x$ and use the other equation:
$$
y''=-y'+3x'=-y'+12x-6y.
$$
Since $3x=y'+y$, this becomes $y''-3y'+2y=0$. Its roots are one and two, so $y=Ae^t+Be^{2t}$. The given data imply $y'(0)=-2+3=1$, hence $A+B=2$ and $A+2B=1$. Thus $A=3$, $B=-1$, giving
$$
\boxed{y(t)=3e^t-e^{2t}}.
$$
Recover the other dependent variable from $x=(y'+y)/3$:
$$
\boxed{x(t)=2e^t-e^{2t}}.
$$
These expressions satisfy both original equations and the two initial values.

