The <Euclidean distance> is $D(x,y)=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$. Along the <parametric curve>,
$$
D^2=a^2\cosh^2t+b^2\sinh^2t
=a^2+(a^2+b^2)\sinh^2t.
$$
This is at least $a^2$, with equality exactly when $t=0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{(x_m,y_m)=(a,0),\qquad D_{\min}=a}.
$$

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <hyperbolic functions> identity eliminates $t$ from the <parametric curve>:
$$
\boxed{\frac{x^2}{a^2}-\frac{y^2}{b^2}=1,\qquad x\geq a}.
$$
The restriction is essential: $\cosh t\geq1$, so the parametrization traces only the right branch of the full <hyperbola>. Conversely, every point on that right branch is reached by taking $t=\operatorname{arsinh}(y/b)$.

= c
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Solving for $y$ gives $y=\pm(b/a)\sqrt{x^2-a^2}$ with $x\geq a$. For large $x$, the <square root> is $x-a^2/(2x)+O(x^{-3})$, so the two <asymptotes> are
$$
\boxed{y=\frac ba x,\qquad y=-\frac ba x}.
$$
The curve lies between these lines on the right side and has its nearest point, with vertical tangent, at $(a,0)$. As $t$ increases it travels from the lower unbounded end through that vertex to the upper unbounded end. The left <hyperbola> branch is not part of the given curve.

\Image[paper-2-hyperbola.png]
{title=The parametrized right branch of a hyperbola, its foci, nearest point, and asymptotes}

= d
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Put $c=\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$ and denote the distances to $(-c,0)$ and $(c,0)$ by $d_-$ and $d_+$. Direct expansion using $c^2=a^2+b^2$ gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
d_-^2&=(a\cosh t+c)^2+b^2\sinh^2t=(c\cosh t+a)^2,\\
d_+^2&=(a\cosh t-c)^2+b^2\sinh^2t=(c\cosh t-a)^2.
\end{aligned}
$$
Since $c>a>0$ and $\cosh t\geq1$, both expressions inside the final square roots are positive. Hence
$$
\boxed{d_--d_+=2a}
$$
for every point of this right branch. This is the <focal-distance identity for a hyperbola>; on the left branch the signed difference would reverse, while its absolute value would remain $2a$.

