Differentiate the <scalar field> with respect to each Cartesian coordinate:
$$
\boxed{\nabla V=(6x^2,2y-1,6z)}.
$$
In particular its value at the point used below is $(24,3,-6)$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <cylinder> is a <level set> of $h=x^2+y^2-8$, so its outward <normal vector> is parallel to $\nabla h=(2x,2y,0)$. At the point this is $(4,4,0)$, with norm $4\sqrt2$. Choosing the outward <orientation> gives
$$
\boxed{\hat{\mathbf n}=\frac{(1,1,0)}{\sqrt2}}.
$$
Its negative is the equally valid inward <unit normal vector>; the next <directional derivative> then changes sign.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use the <gradient> value from (i) and the outward <normal vector> from (ii). The rate of change per unit distance is
$$
\boxed{D_{\hat{\mathbf n}}V=(24,3,-6)\cdot\frac{(1,1,0)}{\sqrt2}
=\frac{27}{\sqrt2}}.
$$

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Put $R=\sqrt8$ and parametrize the circle counterclockwise, as viewed from positive $z$, by $\mathbf r(\phi)=(R\cos\phi,R\sin\phi,3)$ for $0\leq\phi\leq2\pi$. The restricted field is
$$
V(\phi)=2R^3\cos^3\phi+R^2\sin^2\phi+27-R\sin\phi.
$$
**The PDF does not specify the differential in this <line integral>.** An oriented <vector line integral weighted by a scalar field> uses $d\mathbf r=(-R\sin\phi,R\cos\phi,0)\,d\phi$. Its two nonzero components are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\oint_\Gamma V\,dx&=-R\int_0^{2\pi}V(\phi)\sin\phi\,d\phi=\pi R^2,\\
\oint_\Gamma V\,dy&=R\int_0^{2\pi}V(\phi)\cos\phi\,d\phi
=2R^4\int_0^{2\pi}\cos^4\phi\,d\phi=\frac32\pi R^4.
\end{aligned}
$$
All other terms vanish by sine-cosine symmetry. Thus this interpretation gives
$$
\boxed{\oint_\Gamma V\,d\mathbf r=(8\pi,96\pi,0)}.
$$

If the intended convention is the scalar <arc length> <integral>, then $ds=R\,d\phi$ and
$$
\oint_\Gamma V\,ds=R\left(R^2\int_0^{2\pi}\sin^2\phi\,d\phi+54\pi\right)
=\boxed{124\sqrt2\,\pi}.
$$
That scalar <integral> is independent of traversal direction. Giving both values retains the actual distinction left unspecified by the wording.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Assume the components of $\mathbf F$ have continuous second <partial derivatives>. Expanding the <divergence> of its <curl> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\nabla\cdot(\nabla\times\mathbf F)
={}&\partial_x\partial_yF_z-\partial_x\partial_zF_y
+\partial_y\partial_zF_x-\partial_y\partial_xF_z\\
&+\partial_z\partial_xF_y-\partial_z\partial_yF_x.
\end{aligned}
$$
Each positive term cancels a negative term because the corresponding mixed <derivatives> commute. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\nabla\cdot\mathbf G=\nabla\cdot(\nabla\times\mathbf F)=0}.
$$

