= Solution
{parent=a}

Along a differentiable graph satisfying the implicit equation, the <multivariable chain rule> gives
$$
\frac{d}{dx}F(x,y(x))=F_x+F_y\frac{dy}{dx}=0.
$$
Thus, wherever $F_y\ne0$,
$$
\boxed{\frac{dy}{dx}=-\frac{F_x}{F_y}}.
$$
The <implicit function theorem> supplies such a local graph under that nonvanishing condition. At a <singular point> both numerator and denominator vanish, so this division cannot determine its tangent.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the specified <polynomial>,
$$
F_y=2(y-x^2),\qquad F_x=-4x(y-x^2)-5x^4.
$$
The first equation $F_y=0$ forces $y=x^2$; substitution into $F_x=0$ then gives $x=0$, and hence $y=0$. It also satisfies the curve equation. Therefore the unique <singular point> is
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=(0,0)}.
$$

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A real square on the left requires $x^5\geq0$, so $x\geq0$. Taking the two square roots gives exactly the two real graph branches
$$
\boxed{y_+(x)=x^2+x^{5/2},\qquad y_-(x)=x^2-x^{5/2},\qquad x\geq0}.
$$
They meet only at the origin. The local coordinate change $Y=y-x^2$ turns the equation into $Y^2=x^5$, the <plane cusp of type (2,5)>; both branches share the tangent $y=0$ there.

= d
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For $x>0$, differentiate the two explicit branches:
$$
y_\pm'=x\left(2\pm\frac52\sqrt x\right),\qquad
 y_\pm''=2\pm\frac{15}{4}\sqrt x.
$$
The plus branch is strictly increasing and strictly <convex> for $x>0$. Its only zero is $x=0$, also its one-sided <global minimum>; it has no interior extremum or <inflection point>.

The minus branch rises initially, then has $y_-'=0$ at $\sqrt x=4/5$, changing from positive to negative. Its second <derivative> changes sign at $\sqrt x=8/15$. Its zeros follow from $y_-=x^2(1-\sqrt x)$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
&y_+&y_-\\\hline
\text{zeros}&0&0,\ 1\\
\text{interior maximum}&\text{none}&16/25\\
\text{interior minimum}&\text{none}&\text{none}\\
\text{inflection}&\text{none}&64/225
\end{array}}
$$
The origin is a one-sided <local minimum> of both graph branches, because their domain ends there; it is not an interior <critical point>. The minus branch has no <global minimum> since it tends to $-\infty$ as $x\to\infty$. Its maximum and inflection heights, useful for the sketch, are $256/3125$ and $28672/759375$, respectively.

= e
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The upper branch leaves the origin horizontally and curves upward, growing like $x^{5/2}$. The lower branch shares the horizontal tangent, rises to its small maximum at $x=16/25$, then crosses the axis at $x=1$ and falls toward $-\infty$. Its <concavity> changes at $x=64/225$, before the maximum. A separate zoom makes those nearby lower-branch features visible.

