= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Under the regularity and integrability conditions for the <Leibniz integral rule>,
$$
\boxed{\frac{d}{d\theta}\int_{a(\theta)}^{b(\theta)}f(x,\theta)\,dx
=f(b(\theta),\theta)b'(\theta)-f(a(\theta),\theta)a'(\theta)
+\int_{a(\theta)}^{b(\theta)}\frac{\partial f}{\partial\theta}(x,\theta)\,dx}.
$$
The first two terms account for the moving endpoints; the last accounts for the integrand's own parameter dependence.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $m>-1$, integrate the base <power> to obtain $J(m)=\int_0^1x^m\,dx=1/(m+1)$. Since $\partial_m^n x^m=x^m(\log x)^n$, differentiating $n$ times gives
$$
\boxed{\int_0^1x^m(\log x)^n\,dx
=\frac{d^n}{dm^n}\frac1{m+1}
=\frac{(-1)^nn!}{(m+1)^{n+1}}}.
$$
Here $n$ is a nonnegative integer. For justification, keep $m$ in a small closed interval bounded below by $m_0>-1$. The differentiated integrands are dominated by $x^{m_0}|\log x|^n$, whose <integral> becomes $\int_0^\infty e^{-(m_0+1)t}t^n\,dt$ under $x=e^{-t}$ and is finite. The <dominated convergence theorem> establishes the needed <differentiation under the integral sign> and the <logarithmic moments of a power on the unit interval>.

= c
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use <differentiation under the integral sign>, justified by the integrable bound $|x\sin(tx)e^{-x^2/2}|\leq xe^{-x^2/2}$. This gives
$$
F'(t)=-\int_0^\infty x\sin(tx)e^{-x^2/2}\,dx
=\int_0^\infty\sin(tx)\,d(e^{-x^2/2}).
$$
<Integration by parts> makes the <boundary> term vanish: <sine> vanishes at zero and the Gaussian tends to zero at infinity. Therefore
$$
\boxed{F'(t)=-t\int_0^\infty\cos(tx)e^{-x^2/2}\,dx=-tF(t)}.
$$
The <integrating factor> $e^{t^2/2}$ makes $(e^{t^2/2}F)'=0$, so $F(t)=Ce^{-t^2/2}$. At $t=0$, the given half-line <Gaussian integral> fixes $C=\sqrt{\pi/2}$. Thus
$$
\boxed{F(t)=\sqrt{\frac\pi2}\,e^{-t^2/2}}.
$$
This is the even cosine-transform form of the <Fourier transform of a Gaussian>.
